Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["InfraParallelQ", "ObjectName",
 CellID->1224892054,ExpressionUUID->"8393d601-4014-44ba-afc9-60862df940ec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell["   ", "ModInfo",ExpressionUUID->"39cc637c-3fdb-4a98-b169-5de90a4b72e8"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraParallelQ"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/InfraParallelQ", 
     "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["l1", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["l2", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "50ff3627-259e-40d9-a348-a5127ffafce4"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "tests whether the two vertex sets are parallel: disjoint, and at constant \
distance from each other.",
 "\n"
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"64e88d01-2a46-4a8c-96b2-93ce1309a24b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Definition: ",
 StyleBox["l1", "TI"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["l2", "TI"],
 " are parallel when they are disjoint and every vertex of ",
 StyleBox["l2", "TI"],
 " is at the same distance from the set ",
 StyleBox["l1", "TI"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"9a901b95-9cff-4c68-beef-c538dffc971d"],

Cell["\<\
Both conditions matter, and each corresponds to something classical. \
Disjointness is Euclid's Definition 23, that parallel lines do not meet. \
Constant distance is the level-set reading, which is the only thing a metric \
can say without a notion of direction.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"f0f921ea-70c4-4185-8746-37f31ed70e67"],

Cell["Two consequences that catch people out.", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"9277566e-932f-4280-9ba3-9101bff6d8bf"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["A line is not parallel to itself.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " It meets itself everywhere, so disjointness fails and the answer is ",
 Cell[BoxData["False"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "90373eb1-bbd2-4d0e-abca-c42a1baf0576"],
 ". Parallelism here is not reflexive."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7e8df184-3f1f-419a-8698-374af9bf6b9d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Sharing a single vertex is enough to fail.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " Two sets at otherwise constant distance return ",
 Cell[BoxData["False"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "77c4af9e-96a8-4433-becf-a09c5cec78b8"],
 " as soon as they touch."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a8037808-f188-4d5c-8278-c407d5e0fcd1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The predicate does not require its arguments to be lines. It tests two \
vertex sets, so it accepts things that are not paths at all. Concentric \
shells are the clearest case: the shell at radius 3 is parallel to the shell \
at radius 2, at constant distance 1. If you need the arguments to be maximal \
geodesics, test that separately with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraLineQ"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/InfraLineQ", 
    "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "2bc91e0b-ef62-4e7f-a8f5-231a47f3dfc1"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"2345aff1-194b-4fdc-ad8d-6c4e2c6d556a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "That is the difference from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["FindInfraParallel"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry/ref/FindInfraParallel", 
    "WolframInstitute/SyntheticInfrageometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "7c346e58-e213-4ee4-b98a-13ae5f756ea4"],
 ", and it is a real one. The finder returns only ",
 StyleBox["maximal geodesics", "TI"],
 " at constant distance, and on a lattice it typically finds none. This \
predicate can still return ",
 Cell[BoxData["True"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "01f14d3f-3292-4ee3-9ef0-d559bbbac39d"],
 " for sets at constant distance. A parallel can exist as a set while no \
parallel exists as a line."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"e5eacea5-c54c-4981-848c-87a5eabfd918"],

Cell["Corresponding notions in the classical axiom systems:", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"5bcf88eb-1a6f-4b7c-a3ea-a87cfe0127c5"],

